The plan is written: two task files in session_plan/, plus issue_responses.md. The commit step did nothing because session_plan/ is gitignored. The plan's || true allows for that, so I take it the harness reads the files straight from disk.

Task 1 — Part of #1002 (product, security): Check whether a skill sitting in a directory blocked by --deny-dir still reaches the provider.
• The check captures the actual request body using the stub provider in tests/print_stdout_contract.rs. --print-system-prompt can't answer this: an unfenced skill also gave 0 hits there.
• The same setup with no fence has to show the planted canary text. If it doesn't, the capture is wrong and the task claims nothing about the fence.
• If the skill does leak, the skill loader gets the same check the instruction and memory loaders already use, with a near-miss test and a positive control.
• If it doesn't leak, the deliverable is the test plus the measured numbers. The conclusion is worded narrowly: skill content under a denied dir doesn't reach the request.
• #1002 stays open either way, because the --safe-mode goal-file decision is still undecided.

Task 2 — Part of #869 (product): After /cd, the new directory's [directories] deny entries already block read_file, but @path mentions don't honour them yet.
• `@path, /add, /explain and --image` currently check only the fence set at startup.
• The fix routes their shared check through the existing function the file tools use, rather than copying it.
• When the /cd deny list is empty, behaviour must stay byte-identical.
• The write-up has to say plainly that 5 of the 6 measured readers still see only the startup fence.

